    {
      "text": "### Background on Ama Owusu\n\n**Ama Owusu** was born in Kumasi, Ghana, in 1990 and has a rich academic background in literature. She studied English and Linguistics at the University of Ghana, obtaining her BA in 2012, and later completed an MFA in Creative Writing at the University of East Anglia in 2015. Owusu's short fiction has appeared in prominent outlets, including Granta and Transition Magazine. Her debut novel, **Glassy-eyed**, published in 2023 by Cassava Press, has been longlisted for the Etisalat Prize for Literature. \n\n**Related Interview Insights:**\nOwusu grew up near a glass workshop in Kumasi, which inspired her novel. She discusses her motivations for exploring multigenerational narratives and the unique perspectives she wishes to present about contemporary African fiction in her work.\n\nFor more detailed insights on her background, you can read her profile on [Cassava Press](https://openbooks.net/authors/ama-owusu) and her interview with Brittle Paper [here](https://openbooks.net/interviews/ama-owusu).\n\n### About the Book: Glassy-eyed\n\n**Glassy-eyed** is a debut literary novel set in contemporary Accra, focusing on the **Boateng-Asante** family across three generations and their glass-blowing workshop founded by the patriarch, **Kofi Boateng**, in the 1960s. The narrative navigates three distinct time periods: the workshop's founding years, the struggles of Kofi's daughter **Abena** during the 1990s amid economic shifts, and the present-day challenges faced by Abena's daughter, **Nana**, as she inherits the workshop along with its burdens.\n\nKey themes explored in the novel include:\n- Multigenerational family dynamics\n- The significance of inheritance and craftsmanship\n- The socio-economic landscape of Accra\n- The metaphor of glass in depicting fragility and legacy\n- Women's experiences in economically precarious environments\n\nThe novel's prose is described as precise and unhurried, reflecting on the textures of craft work and familial obligations as Ghana undergoes rapid transformation. The ISBN for this novel is 978-1-73625-441-8, and it contains approximately 87,000 words.\n\nThis combination of personal and cultural narrative creates a rich tapestry that reveals both the beauty and challenges of familial and societal legacies in Ghana.",
