{
  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "Here is an excerpt from *Glassy-eyed* by Ama Owusu, containing the opening section from Chapter 1:\n\n---\n\n**Chapter 1**\n\nThe workshop smelled of sand and heat and something else — something mineral, a smell that had no common name but that Nana had associated, since childhood, with the moment before things changed form. Her grandfather called it the smell of possibility, which she had always found romantic to the point of embarrassment. After twenty years, she simply called it home.\n\nShe arrived in the early morning, before anyone else, the way she always arrived on days she needed to think. The furnace had been banked overnight and the glass pots still held the remnants of yesterday's work — a green that her grandfather had mixed in the 1970s from a formula written in a notebook she could no longer read, because he had written it in a Twi script that mixed two dialects in a way only he had spoken. She had had it translated once, years ago, when he was still alive and could correct the translator's mistakes. She had the corrected version somewhere. The formula still worked. She did not fully understand why.\n\nThe workshop was arranged the same way it had been arranged in 1963, when Kofi Boateng had built it on a plot of rented land in what was then the outer edge of Kumasi. The furnace at the back, the glory hole to its left, the marver table to the right, the bench running the length of the north wall where finished pieces cooled in sequence of their making. Her grandmother's hand was in the arrangement; Kofi had told Nana this more than once, as if it mattered that the spatial logic of the workshop had been, from the beginning, a collaboration. Her grandmother had been a trader, not a maker. She had understood the importance of the movement of bodies through space, and had seen, in Kofi's first rough sketch of the workshop floor, something that could be improved.\n\nNana set her bag on the bench and stood for a moment at the entrance, looking in. The light at this hour came through the north window at a low angle and caught the finished pieces on their stands in a way that made each one briefly remarkable. This was the time of day she felt, most acutely, the weight of the thing she had agreed to continue.\n\n---\n\nThis excerpt provides a glimpse into the world of the Boateng family and their glass-blowing workshop, which serves as an essential setting for the generational narrative. You can use this prose for quoting in your review. If you need more specific sections or additional chapters, please let me know!",
